#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f13faa1-be04-516c-b8c8-1b475c261afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355987} {:answer/id #uuid "985354db-aab4-569f-abdb-a04db389ad3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985354db-aab4-569f-abdb-a04db389ad3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355988} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6bf432-fcef-5d5c-a367-5047cd0ec924" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b117fef-9e1e-5bc1-8b1c-bfb4109c98b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-278083" :question/problem "The $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $81$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $10\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{5}=81$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{5-1} \\\\ 81 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{81}{16}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $10\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >10\\,000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >625 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈16.877.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $17$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $10\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "618879e2-79a5-5f42-add0-dcc305d0d08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "618879e2-79a5-5f42-add0-dcc305d0d08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355991} :answer-field/id #uuid "1408d7e6-4ab7-505c-aa5f-e2eca124668f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef6815fb-0f6d-5589-bcfd-cadc0145d0fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-278089" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $2\\,304$, and the $6$th term is $36\\,864.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $496\\,800$ is the{{field-1}} th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and ${a}_{6}=36\\,864$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{36\\,864}{2\\,304} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and  $r=4$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{4-1} \\\\ 2\\,304 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,304 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =36\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $496\\,800.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >496\\,800 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >13\\,800 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈7.876.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $496\\,800.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f2ba960-5c63-5d47-8ddf-5a5d1378b3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $33$rd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355994} {:answer/id #uuid "83764edb-7364-51cf-92c6-979103a11600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $28$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355995} {:answer/id #uuid "49f5a01d-ffef-53a8-82e5-f1801fab0a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $31$st term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355996} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f5b5b9-10d9-526c-a963-7592f4529187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $29$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355997} {:answer/id #uuid "71a53ab3-8f97-5ada-95a5-03e5e3640e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $30$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71a53ab3-8f97-5ada-95a5-03e5e3640e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $30$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355998} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15c552f-65d4-55ac-8ad4-877c643eb48a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd214d9a-0991-5f60-9033-e3b27bd16696" :question/math-academy-id "q-39647" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,2$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n18,\\,12,\\,8,\\,\\frac{16}{3},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=18$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{12}{18}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,2.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ 18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,2}{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1≈29.159\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $30$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0b1cdb4-29f1-5dc0-ab23-c6457479c2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $42$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356001} {:answer/id #uuid "5325ed31-0f99-5215-a287-dd1376b00ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $39$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356002} {:answer/id #uuid "049fcf9e-c6cd-552e-a56a-4ccd5e1f0fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $41$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356003} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2f8df5-3daf-5db3-a4bb-c853206ac374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356004} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1dc434-81fc-5bf1-9afb-edf94074adac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $38$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d2f8df5-3daf-5db3-a4bb-c853206ac374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356004} :answer-field/id #uuid "e05c44cf-98fe-594d-b145-a389f2f62043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "954f45fc-f0a3-5593-bd6c-65e4d48ff69e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39648" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1f7d28-3d48-5132-ad94-fc02346a76e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $7$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090300} {:answer/id #uuid "9d23524b-a755-5488-8c64-8fab447a8379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $3$rd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090301} {:answer/id #uuid "26ab63c2-9755-57d8-883c-39507d5dd71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $6$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090302} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbdc32c-6d81-5a49-a6d4-4fcff7d632a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $4$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090303} {:answer/id #uuid "49b36c77-e751-5b86-8374-e0e55aa558af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $5$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49b36c77-e751-5b86-8374-e0e55aa558af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $5$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f62dd85-b08c-5a2c-b913-e4fdb4e47f4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1304178d-7d7c-51c3-94a2-a4ae1963dcc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-39651" :question/problem "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $75$, and the common ratio is $5.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,713?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=75$ and  $r=5$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{3-1} \\\\ 75 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 75 & =25{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,713.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{n-1} & >1\\,713 \\\\ {5}^{n-1} & >571 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{5}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1≈4.944\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,713.$"}]}
